Radical innovation (advisory, for the operator to weigh)

Release-matrix fixtures built by the releases' own DuckDB. Today every old-warehouse fixture comes from DuckDB 2.0.0 writing v1.0.0 storage, with the 2.0 build of vss serializing the HNSW index. Users' files were written by DuckDB 1.5.4 and its vss. The drop without vss on a real 1.5.4 file is proven only at schema 8 (this machine's warehouse and the Mac shadow test). Structural sketch:
- The gate's released_sql_files(repo, tag) becomes the single reader of "what shipped".
- A session-scoped fixture released_warehouse(tag) runs, for each distinct schema head among the v* tags (v0.1.0 gives 4 and v2.1.3 gives 8), a child `uv run --no-project --with duckdb==1.5.4 python tests/_build_release.py <sql-dir> <db>`. The child loads vss, applies that tag's migration blobs with the runner's semantics, writes rows and embeddings, and closes.
- W6 is then parametrized over tags instead of over 2.0-built versions 3 to 8. Schema 3, which no release left as its head, keeps the 2.0-built child.
This proves acceptance criterion 10 ("as any earlier release left it") with the real on-disk format. It also stops the in-tree migration copies from being the fixture source. Cost: CI downloads the duckdb 1.5.4 wheel and vss once (cacheable), and each tag build takes a few seconds. Memory confirms that a nested `uv run --with duckdb==VERSION` selects that version.
